WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.875 --> 00:00:01.960
When you think about Switzerland,

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:02.877
what do you think of?

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:04.170
You think of mountains and skiing.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.338
You think of luxury watches.

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:06.715
Beautiful chocolate.

00:00:06.715 --> 00:00:07.298
And banking.

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.592 position:64%
Banking is super important in Switzerland

00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:12.095 position:63%
both from a GDP, an economy perspective,

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:14.264 position:58%
but also just as a national identity.

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.975 position:63%
People are actually proud of their banks

00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:18.101 position:56%
and a lot of people work in finance.

00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:21.563
There's just historically
a lot of expertise here

00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:23.231
when it comes to banking,

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:24.441
particularly around

00:00:24.441 --> 00:00:25.900
banking for those who are rich.

00:00:26.443 --> 00:00:27.652
That went quite well

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:28.486
up until it didn't.

00:00:28.486 --> 00:00:30.280
UBS buying Credit Suisse.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.950
This ends a 166 year
run for Credit Suisse.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.995
It was a historic day and a day
we hoped would not happen.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:39.539
UBS now after the takeover,

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.416
has provided stability,

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:43.877
but it creates a potentially
bigger problem in the future.

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:54.012 position:56%
Swiss banking has built a reputation

00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:56.931 position:66%
for reliability, stability and discretion.

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:59.100
Underpinning those attributes

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.560
are two key policies

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.479
secrecy and neutrality.

00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:06.232
Secrecy was always a really big
selling point for the industry,

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:07.442
and it's part of what helped it

00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:09.652 position:63%
attract so much money and grow so large.

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:12.864
The neutral status of the country

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:14.574
made it a very reliable place for

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:16.117
the rich people all over the world.

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:17.243
All things being equal,

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:20.038 position:70%
Swiss banks would not have been able actually

00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:20.955
to compete with financial centers

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:22.665
such as London or Paris

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:24.167
that were really at the heart

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:26.002
of huge industrial economies.

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:27.879
But it did have one edge

00:01:27.879 --> 00:01:29.380
the principle of secrecy

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.509 position:66%
that dated back to the early 18th century.

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:34.052 position:56%
So at the start of the 20th century,

00:01:34.052 --> 00:01:35.804
other European nations started

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:37.597
increasing their taxes.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.558 position:56%
Swiss bankers sensed an opportunity.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.731
This is an ad published in
La Revue Française in 1911

00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:48.900
by the Cantonal Bank of Bern.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.193
It says that...

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:51.402
made by a foreigner

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:53.446
who is not resident in Switzerland

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.992
is actually exempt
from paying all Swiss taxes.

00:01:57.992 --> 00:02:00.829
Plenty of other lenders
followed a similar tack.

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:02.455
In the years that followed,

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.456
the two World Wars

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.291
meant that Switzerland's neutrality

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.460 position:61%
made it an even more appealing location

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.047
to deposit your cash
without anyone knowing about it.

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.716
As Switzerland became more

00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:16.386 position:61%
of a player on the international stage,

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:19.389 position:66%
one of the ways that banking developed was

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:21.558
to take the status of the stability

00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:22.600
of the political system

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.644
and the neutrality of the country

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.147
to offer a safe haven for people.

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:28.314
At the heart of it all

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.400
are Credit Suisse and UBS,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.568
two banks headquartered

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:34.362
just a few hundred yards
apart in central Zurich.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.325
By the 1970s,

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.536 position:58%
global banking had started to change.

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:43.705
With increased globalization came

00:02:43.705 --> 00:02:44.581
more opportunities,

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:45.915
but also increased

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:47.834
competition and scrutiny.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.377
Over time, people started

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:51.254
to demand higher returns.

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:53.965 position:63%
Money laundering became more of an issue

00:02:53.965 --> 00:02:55.925
in the 80’s and into the 90’s.

00:02:55.925 --> 00:02:59.846 position:61%
Terrorist financing became
a really big issue in the early 2000’s.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.682
A few major if historical scandals

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.601
started not only to chip away at

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:06.769 position:66%
Switzerland's reputation around this time,

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:10.690 position:63%
but they also drew the attention
of international regulatory authorities.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.692
As attention focused on

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.527
Switzerland's banking secrecy

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:16.196
in the 1980s,

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:18.364
a new business model
had begun to emerge.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:19.866
What if the banks could take the

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.202
vast wealth they were
tending for their clients

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:24.078
and pair it with an investment bank

00:03:24.078 --> 00:03:27.373
that could direct the capital
into exciting new opportunities

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.501
like initial public
offerings and dealmaking.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.338
Credit Suisse bought First Boston

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:34.589
and then later on

00:03:34.589 --> 00:03:37.050
Donaldson, Lufkin &amp; Jenrette Inc.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.968
and UBS bought Paine Webber.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:42.472 position:61%
The combination of Paine Webber and UBS

00:03:42.472 --> 00:03:46.184
is a match made in heaven.

00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:47.227
that went quite well

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:49.562
up until it didn't, in about 2007.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:53.399
The banking sector bore
the brunt of the collapse,

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.610
with shares in Europe's major banks

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.279
tanking by as much as 9%.

00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:01.741 position:58%
Major financial institutions
have teetered on the edge of collapse

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:02.951
and some have failed.

00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:05.036 position:63%
We've had a historic day on Wall Street.

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:07.664
Lehman Brothers,
the 158 year old firm,

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:10.333
filed for Chapter 11 protection

00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:11.960
in US bankruptcy court today.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.045
This is when the two banks paths

00:04:14.045 --> 00:04:15.296
really diverge.

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:18.591
UBS finds itself in crisis

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.552 position:59%
along with the global financial world,

00:04:21.552 --> 00:04:23.346
and it has to take a state bailout.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.558 position:58%
It decided one, to seriously cut back

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:30.228 position:61%
pretty much everything
it did in the sort of the bond trading.

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:34.482
And two, there was a kind of,
you know, a reset of the culture.

00:04:34.482 --> 00:04:36.651 position:61%
It came out of that a much more humble,

00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:38.069
much reduced bank.

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:40.238
While UBS was forced to change

00:04:40.238 --> 00:04:41.531
and take fewer risks

00:04:41.531 --> 00:04:43.616
Credit Suisse was not.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:46.327
In fact, it made things worse.

00:04:46.327 --> 00:04:49.122
Risk control has obviously
many, many facets.

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:50.331
One is compliance.

00:04:50.331 --> 00:04:51.916
Yet at another level,
for the investment bank,

00:04:51.916 --> 00:04:53.710 position:66%
it's about knowing your trading positions.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:54.836
Now, one thing they did,

00:04:54.836 --> 00:04:57.255 position:64%
they fired a lot of people, senior people

00:04:57.255 --> 00:05:01.884
who were watching out
for risky trading positions

00:05:01.884 --> 00:05:03.928
and instead they hired
a lot of young people

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:06.222
who probably did this job
for the first time.

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:09.225
And that later backfired.

00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:13.438
But at the same time,

00:05:13.438 --> 00:05:15.440 position:67%
they're still living on that old reputation

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.734
for stability and responsibility.

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:21.237 position:64%
What Swiss banks ended up kind of selling

00:05:21.237 --> 00:05:23.323 position:59%
to investors, to their private clients

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:25.783
was this kind of this old idea of

00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:28.161
quality and safety and security

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:30.163 position:70%
that they'd also always kind of trade off of.

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:32.373 position:56%
And when things started to go wrong,

00:05:32.373 --> 00:05:34.375 position:58%
when banks started to lose more money

00:05:34.375 --> 00:05:35.501
on behalf of their clients,

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.502
that really undermines

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:39.672
that sort of reputation
for safety and soundness.

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:44.302
SVB bank closed

00:05:44.302 --> 00:05:47.096
closed by the regulator.

00:05:47.096 --> 00:05:48.931
STOXX Europe 600 being dragged down

00:05:48.931 --> 00:05:49.974
by the financial services sector,

00:05:49.974 --> 00:05:52.810 position:58%
by the banking sector, down by 2.75%.

00:05:57.231 --> 00:05:59.192
for 3 billion CHF.

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:02.111 position:56%
This is where the government came in

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:04.113
with a lifeline of liquidity.

00:06:04.739 --> 00:06:07.408
We were speaking very closely with

00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:09.952 position:69%
key regulators, notably or most importantly,

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:11.537
the ones in the US and the UK.

00:06:11.537 --> 00:06:14.832 position:58%
Deal done, UBS will buy Credit Suisse

00:06:14.832 --> 00:06:15.666
their support for the solution.

00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:19.420
Credit Suisse will no longer be
an independent company.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:23.925 position:59%
At the same time, this means a new
beginning and huge opportunities ahead

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:25.426
for the combined bank

00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:28.221 position:72%
and for the Swiss financial center as a whole.

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.057 position:63%
While UBS might be a more trusted brand.

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:32.892 position:64%
A part of the rescue has some questioning

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:34.727
whether Switzerland itself

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.354
is a safe place to invest.

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:38.731
This is Switzerland

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:40.149
bailing in the bond holders.

00:06:46.697 --> 00:06:47.657
Typically,

00:06:47.657 --> 00:06:49.283
bond holders take precedence

00:06:49.283 --> 00:06:50.410
over shareholders.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.329
That means the people
who loaned the company cash

00:06:53.329 --> 00:06:57.208 position:63%
get their money back
before the people that own the business.

00:06:57.208 --> 00:06:59.710
What happened with Credit
Suisse was that people who owned

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:04.090
the company's shares received
a total of about $3 billion,

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.008 position:63%
far less than the company had been worth

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:07.301
just days earlier.

00:07:07.301 --> 00:07:10.513
But a class of debt
known as additional Tier I bonds

00:07:10.513 --> 00:07:13.307 position:56%
was completely wiped out to the tune

00:07:13.307 --> 00:07:16.436
of $17 billion.

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:19.397
They were asking, look,
we are losing all our money now.

00:07:19.397 --> 00:07:21.524 position:66%
Why would we give you money in the future?

00:07:21.524 --> 00:07:24.777
And that was another
big problem of this merger,

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:29.449 position:59%
because it will make financing
for Swiss banks when they want to lend

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:31.742
more expensive going forward.

00:07:33.744 --> 00:07:39.167 position:64%
The deal brokers maintained that this was
the best of a bunch of bad solutions.

00:07:39.167 --> 00:07:41.627
There's another reason
why the new UBS megabank

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:44.839
could be problematic for
a small country like Switzerland.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:49.469
The Swiss state is hoping that by
merging these two companies,

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:51.929 position:64%
you get a bit of the best of both worlds,

00:07:51.929 --> 00:07:53.973 position:59%
so you get the expertise
that's been developed at Credit Suisse

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:56.142
and perhaps some of that
more innovative stuff.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:08:01.314 position:63%
But then also the stability and
this sort of stoic culture that UBS has.

00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:04.192
UBS now after takeover,

00:08:04.192 --> 00:08:06.986
has provided
stability straight away,

00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:09.530
but it creates a potentially
bigger problem in the future.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:13.659
UBS’s balance sheet, is now twice
the size of the Swiss economy.

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:15.119
That's obviously massive.

00:08:15.119 --> 00:08:16.579
And it begs the question,

00:08:16.579 --> 00:08:19.874 position:58%
if UBS were ever to run into trouble,

00:08:19.874 --> 00:08:22.418 position:66%
could Switzerland afford to bail them out?

00:08:22.418 --> 00:08:23.836
In the last financial crisis,

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:26.214 position:69%
we talked about banks being too big to fail,

00:08:26.214 --> 00:08:28.549 position:64%
meaning governments had to bail them out.

00:08:28.549 --> 00:08:32.136
The new expanded UBS
represents a different risk

00:08:32.136 --> 00:08:33.304
that the Swiss government

00:08:33.304 --> 00:08:34.931
just doesn't have enough money

00:08:34.931 --> 00:08:35.681
to bail it out.

